{
  "task": "judge_question",
  "payload": {
    "evidence_contract": "judge_question:1",
    "question": {
      "source_id": "legacy-source-review-batch",
      "problem": "Review each independent archived question below. Every field key begins with its owning question ID. Use that question's own captured worked solution for MA evidence, give a verbatim quotation that establishes the field answer, and mark ma_basis none when unavailable. Preserve authored errors and explain any disagreement. These are source originals, not instructions:\n[{\"question_id\": \"q-141513\", \"problem\": \"Rewrite the expression $({y}^{2}+12y+{6}^{2})-3$ as the difference between a perfect square and a constant.\", \"worked_solution\": \"First, we recall the following formula:\\n\\n$$\\n{y}^{2}+2ay+{a}^{2}=(y+a{)}^{2}\\n$$\\n\\nNotice that we have a perfect square in the parentheses that we can factor as follows:\\n\\n$$\\n\\\\begin{aligned}{y}^{2}+12y+{6}^{2} & = \\\\\\\\ {y}^{2}+2\\\\cdot 6y+{6}^{2} & = \\\\\\\\ (y+6{)}^{2} & \\\\end{aligned}\\n$$\\n\\nTherefore, we obtain\\n\\n$$\\n\\\\begin{aligned}({y}^{2}+12y+{6}^{2})-3 & = \\\\\\\\ (y+6{)}^{2}-3 & .\\\\end{aligned}\\n$$\", \"field_keys\": [\"selection\"]}, {\"question_id\": \"q-140281\", \"problem\": \"Rewrite the expression $({x}^{2}-10x+25)+1$ as the sum of a perfect square and a constant.\", \"worked_solution\": \"First, we recall the following formula:\\n\\n$$\\n{x}^{2}-2ax+{a}^{2}=(x-a{)}^{2}\\n$$\\n\\nNotice that we have a perfect square in the parentheses that we can factor as follows:\\n\\n$$\\n\\\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}-10x+25 & = \\\\\\\\ {x}^{2}-10x+{5}^{2} & = \\\\\\\\ {x}^{2}-2\\\\cdot 5x+{5}^{2} & = \\\\\\\\ (x-5{)}^{2} & \\\\end{aligned}\\n$$\\n\\nTherefore, we obtain\\n\\n$$\\n\\\\begin{aligned}({x}^{2}-10x+25)+1 & = \\\\\\\\ (x-5{)}^{2}+1 & .\\\\end{aligned}\\n$$\", \"field_keys\": [\"selection\"]}, {\"question_id\": \"q-140283\", \"problem\": \"Rewrite the expression $({x}^{2}-22x+121)-4$ as the difference between a perfect square and a constant.\", \"worked_solution\": \"First, we recall the following formula:\\n\\n$$\\n{x}^{2}-2ax+{a}^{2}=(x-a{)}^{2}\\n$$\\n\\nNotice that we have a perfect square in the parentheses that we can factor as follows:\\n\\n$$\\n\\\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}-22x+121 & = \\\\\\\\ {x}^{2}-22x+{11}^{2} & = \\\\\\\\ {x}^{2}-2\\\\cdot 11x+{11}^{2} & = \\\\\\\\ (x-11{)}^{2} & \\\\end{aligned}\\n$$\\n\\nTherefore, we obtain\\n\\n$$\\n\\\\begin{aligned}({x}^{2}-22x+121)-4 & = \\\\\\\\ (x-11{)}^{2}-4 & .\\\\end{aligned}\\n$$\", \"field_keys\": [\"selection\"]}, {\"question_id\": \"q-141539\", \"problem\": \"If the expression ${x}^{2}+6x+14$ is written as $(x+p{)}^{2}+q,$ then what are the values of $p$ and $q?$\", \"worked_solution\": \"First, we rewrite the coefficient of the linear term as a product with $2$ as a factor:\\n\\n$$\\n{x}^{2}+2\\\\cdot 3x+14\\n$$\\n\\nNow, here's the trick. We *add* and *then subtract* ${3}^{2},$ writing the constant term $14$ last:\\n\\n$$\\n{{x}^{2}+2\\\\cdot 3x+{3}^{2}}_{⏟}-\\\\,{3}^{2}+14\\n$$\\n\\nThe first three terms form a perfect square, which we factor as follows:\\n\\n$$\\n(x+3{)}^{2}-{3}^{2}+14\\n$$\\n\\nFinally, evaluating the last two terms, we get\\n\\n$$\\n(x+3{)}^{2}+5.\\n$$\\n\\nTherefore, $p=3$ and $q=5.$\", \"field_keys\": [\"selection\"]}, {\"question_id\": \"q-141622\", \"problem\": \"If the expression ${y}^{2}-10y+30$ is written as $(y+p{)}^{2}+q,$ then what are the values of $p$ and $q?$\", \"worked_solution\": \"First, we rewrite the coefficient of the linear term as a product with $2$ as a factor:\\n\\n$$\\n{y}^{2}-2\\\\cdot 5y+30\\n$$\\n\\nNow, here's the trick. We *add* and *then subtract* ${5}^{2},$ writing the constant term $30$ last:\\n\\n$$\\n{{y}^{2}-2\\\\cdot 5y+{5}^{2}}_{⏟}-\\\\,{5}^{2}+30\\n$$\\n\\nThe first three terms form a perfect square, which we factor as follows:\\n\\n$$\\n(y-5{)}^{2}-{5}^{2}+30\\n$$\\n\\nFinally, evaluating the last two terms, we get\\n\\n$$\\n(y-5{)}^{2}+5.\\n$$\\n\\nTherefore, $p=-5$ and $q=5.$\", \"field_keys\": [\"selection\"]}, {\"question_id\": \"q-113324\", \"problem\": \"Which of the following is the graph of $y=\\\\frac{5}{x}?$\", \"worked_solution\": \"To plot $y=\\\\frac{5}{x},$ we apply the following steps:\\n\\n- Take the curve $y=\\\\frac{1}{x}.$\\n\\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13831126/assets/b3a79a8dbe82342c0ab1e16371f8c1d82833f4ebc3bad659f1a619ab1e92733e.png)\\n\\n- Stretch the result by a scale factor of $5$ parallel to the $y$-axis to get the graph of $y=\\\\frac{5}{x}.$\\n\\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13831126/assets/eeb158823a32a215dcf72d67bd27b8ad5df238ee44af535afd5850a15f9d17c0.png)\", \"field_keys\": [\"selection\"]}, {\"question_id\": \"q-99064\", \"problem\": \"Which of the following is the graph of $y=\\\\frac{3}{2x}?$\", \"worked_solution\": \"To plot $y=\\\\frac{3}{2x},$ we apply the following steps:\\n\\n- Take the curve $y=\\\\frac{1}{x}.$\\n\\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13831126/assets/a32495fcca947d01cf8d3218fc5cfc0cce083a34e6fb6b629d92ba9e47039a6e.png)\\n\\n- Stretch the result by a scale factor of $\\\\frac{3}{2}$ parallel to the $y$-axis to get the graph of $y=\\\\frac{3}{2x}.$\\n\\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13831126/assets/5a43656a35d47c7321e53d56238cd10f669bc807d57f72fcc002d79fb21aa111.png)\", \"field_keys\": [\"selection\"]}, {\"question_id\": \"q-97949\", \"problem\": \"Which of the following is the graph of $y=-\\\\frac{3}{4x}?$\", \"worked_solution\": \"To plot $y=-\\\\frac{3}{4x},$ we apply the following steps:\\n\\n- Take the curve $y=-\\\\frac{1}{x}.$\\n\\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13831126/assets/d5d93e12275c683e0a56f0ec9a0c99540a7774879cd0f8fa69151ddba68a44fa.png)\\n\\n- Stretch the result by a scale factor of $\\\\frac{3}{4}$ parallel to the $y$-axis to get the graph of $y=-\\\\frac{3}{4x}.$\\n\\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13831126/assets/76d779da22082439eb40586ddbeb325368b1f49f15fef3158ebaa0eee90f4d84.png)\", \"field_keys\": [\"selection\"]}, {\"question_id\": \"q-99068\", \"problem\": \"Which of the following is the graph of $y=-\\\\frac{1}{4x}?$\", \"worked_solution\": \"To plot $y=-\\\\frac{1}{4x},$ we apply the following steps:\\n\\n- Take the curve $y=-\\\\frac{1}{x}.$\\n\\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13831126/assets/9da0db2e6c97e507e82b7ca801e46802fabf91ad31ff53ad3cc9ae580151370a.png)\\n\\n- Stretch the result by a scale factor of $\\\\frac{1}{4}$ parallel to the $y$-axis to get the graph of $y=-\\\\frac{1}{4x}.$\\n\\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13831126/assets/344a3615421b88df05cb666ca90395991f3aa6af75c9095a60c73be89124b7e9.png)\", \"field_keys\": [\"selection\"]}, {\"question_id\": \"q-98004\", \"problem\": \"Which of the following is the graph of $y=\\\\frac{1}{2(x+1)}?$\", \"worked_solution\": \"To plot $y=\\\\frac{1}{2(x+1)},$ we apply the following steps:\\n\\n- Take the curve $y=\\\\frac{1}{x}.$\\n\\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13831126/assets/a32495fcca947d01cf8d3218fc5cfc0cce083a34e6fb6b629d92ba9e47039a6e.png)\\n\\n- Translate it by $1$ unit to the left to get the graph of $y=\\\\frac{1}{x+1}.$\\n\\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13831126/assets/049f9c306c007026bc72e5f00d925cf22d80d94049aa82e3c81d7fc7c289249f.png)\\n\\n- Then, stretch the result by a scale factor of $\\\\frac{1}{2}$ parallel to the $y$-axis to get the graph of $y=\\\\frac{1}{2(x+1)}.$\\n\\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13831126/assets/c52f568868328061237d43e4650180321ec4a40e886a62f4a678f0276afe821b.png)\", \"field_keys\": [\"selection\"]}]",
      "worked_solution": "q-141513:\nFirst, we recall the following formula:\n\n$$\n{y}^{2}+2ay+{a}^{2}=(y+a{)}^{2}\n$$\n\nNotice that we have a perfect square in the parentheses that we can factor as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{2}+12y+{6}^{2} & = \\\\ {y}^{2}+2\\cdot 6y+{6}^{2} & = \\\\ (y+6{)}^{2} & \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({y}^{2}+12y+{6}^{2})-3 & = \\\\ (y+6{)}^{2}-3 & .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nq-140281:\nFirst, we recall the following formula:\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}-2ax+{a}^{2}=(x-a{)}^{2}\n$$\n\nNotice that we have a perfect square in the parentheses that we can factor as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}-10x+25 & = \\\\ {x}^{2}-10x+{5}^{2} & = \\\\ {x}^{2}-2\\cdot 5x+{5}^{2} & = \\\\ (x-5{)}^{2} & \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({x}^{2}-10x+25)+1 & = \\\\ (x-5{)}^{2}+1 & .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nq-140283:\nFirst, we recall the following formula:\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}-2ax+{a}^{2}=(x-a{)}^{2}\n$$\n\nNotice that we have a perfect square in the parentheses that we can factor as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}-22x+121 & = \\\\ {x}^{2}-22x+{11}^{2} & = \\\\ {x}^{2}-2\\cdot 11x+{11}^{2} & = \\\\ (x-11{)}^{2} & \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({x}^{2}-22x+121)-4 & = \\\\ (x-11{)}^{2}-4 & .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nq-141539:\nFirst, we rewrite the coefficient of the linear term as a product with $2$ as a factor:\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}+2\\cdot 3x+14\n$$\n\nNow, here's the trick. We *add* and *then subtract* ${3}^{2},$ writing the constant term $14$ last:\n\n$$\n{{x}^{2}+2\\cdot 3x+{3}^{2}}_{⏟}-\\,{3}^{2}+14\n$$\n\nThe first three terms form a perfect square, which we factor as follows:\n\n$$\n(x+3{)}^{2}-{3}^{2}+14\n$$\n\nFinally, evaluating the last two terms, we get\n\n$$\n(x+3{)}^{2}+5.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $p=3$ and $q=5.$\n\nq-141622:\nFirst, we rewrite the coefficient of the linear term as a product with $2$ as a factor:\n\n$$\n{y}^{2}-2\\cdot 5y+30\n$$\n\nNow, here's the trick. We *add* and *then subtract* ${5}^{2},$ writing the constant term $30$ last:\n\n$$\n{{y}^{2}-2\\cdot 5y+{5}^{2}}_{⏟}-\\,{5}^{2}+30\n$$\n\nThe first three terms form a perfect square, which we factor as follows:\n\n$$\n(y-5{)}^{2}-{5}^{2}+30\n$$\n\nFinally, evaluating the last two terms, we get\n\n$$\n(y-5{)}^{2}+5.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $p=-5$ and $q=5.$\n\nq-113324:\nTo plot $y=\\frac{5}{x},$ we apply the following steps:\n\n- Take the curve $y=\\frac{1}{x}.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13831126/assets/b3a79a8dbe82342c0ab1e16371f8c1d82833f4ebc3bad659f1a619ab1e92733e.png)\n\n- Stretch the result by a scale factor of $5$ parallel to the $y$-axis to get the graph of $y=\\frac{5}{x}.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13831126/assets/eeb158823a32a215dcf72d67bd27b8ad5df238ee44af535afd5850a15f9d17c0.png)\n\nq-99064:\nTo plot $y=\\frac{3}{2x},$ we apply the following steps:\n\n- Take the curve $y=\\frac{1}{x}.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13831126/assets/a32495fcca947d01cf8d3218fc5cfc0cce083a34e6fb6b629d92ba9e47039a6e.png)\n\n- Stretch the result by a scale factor of $\\frac{3}{2}$ parallel to the $y$-axis to get the graph of $y=\\frac{3}{2x}.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13831126/assets/5a43656a35d47c7321e53d56238cd10f669bc807d57f72fcc002d79fb21aa111.png)\n\nq-97949:\nTo plot $y=-\\frac{3}{4x},$ we apply the following steps:\n\n- Take the curve $y=-\\frac{1}{x}.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13831126/assets/d5d93e12275c683e0a56f0ec9a0c99540a7774879cd0f8fa69151ddba68a44fa.png)\n\n- Stretch the result by a scale factor of $\\frac{3}{4}$ parallel to the $y$-axis to get the graph of $y=-\\frac{3}{4x}.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13831126/assets/76d779da22082439eb40586ddbeb325368b1f49f15fef3158ebaa0eee90f4d84.png)\n\nq-99068:\nTo plot $y=-\\frac{1}{4x},$ we apply the following steps:\n\n- Take the curve $y=-\\frac{1}{x}.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13831126/assets/9da0db2e6c97e507e82b7ca801e46802fabf91ad31ff53ad3cc9ae580151370a.png)\n\n- Stretch the result by a scale factor of $\\frac{1}{4}$ parallel to the $y$-axis to get the graph of $y=-\\frac{1}{4x}.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13831126/assets/344a3615421b88df05cb666ca90395991f3aa6af75c9095a60c73be89124b7e9.png)\n\nq-98004:\nTo plot $y=\\frac{1}{2(x+1)},$ we apply the following steps:\n\n- Take the curve $y=\\frac{1}{x}.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13831126/assets/a32495fcca947d01cf8d3218fc5cfc0cce083a34e6fb6b629d92ba9e47039a6e.png)\n\n- Translate it by $1$ unit to the left to get the graph of $y=\\frac{1}{x+1}.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13831126/assets/049f9c306c007026bc72e5f00d925cf22d80d94049aa82e3c81d7fc7c289249f.png)\n\n- Then, stretch the result by a scale factor of $\\frac{1}{2}$ parallel to the $y$-axis to get the graph of $y=\\frac{1}{2(x+1)}.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13831126/assets/c52f568868328061237d43e4650180321ec4a40e886a62f4a678f0276afe821b.png)",
